<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Angkor</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Angkor was the capital region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, flourishing from the ninth to the fifteenth century. It contained great temple complexes and an extensive network of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> and canals that supported a large population. Its partial abandonment as the political centre in the fifteenth century is associated with the Khmer Empire's decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/angkor.jpg" alt="Angkor" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hobe / Holger Behr · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karta_AngkorWat.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sukhothai became a tributary from 1378 and was absorbed in 1438, and armies under Borommarachathirat II attacked <b>Angkor</b>, traditionally in 1431, hastening the Khmer court's move south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 803 they seized Nghệ An from the Tang, and in June 1177 Jaya Indravarman IV's surprise naval assault plundered <b>Angkor</b>, killed the Khmer king and began a four-year occupation of Cambodia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After Jayavarman I the kingdom broke into principalities; his daughter Queen Jayadevi ruled at <b>Angkor</b> and lamented &quot;bad times&quot; on 5 April 713, and the Tang histories say that after 6 February 707 Chenla split into Land Chenla, also called Wendan or Polou, and Water Chenla.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chenla">Chenla · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The École française d’Extrême-Orient, founded in 1900, led the study and restoration of <b>Angkor</b> and the scholarship of the region’s languages and religions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Early consolidation involved the integration of competing chiefdoms through both military force and ritual authority, and the capital settled at <b>Angkor</b> under Yasovarman I in the late ninth century, laying the foundation for one of the most enduring and architecturally significant civilisations in Southeast Asian history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Lao chronicles, Fa Ngum's family was exiled and he grew up at the Khmer court at <b>Angkor</b>, where he married a Khmer princess.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-xang">Lan Xang · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
